---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const APP = "https://login.form4dev.com";
const title = "Formspree alternatives in 2026: an honest comparison from someone building one";
const description =
  "If you're shopping for a Formspree alternative, here's the landscape — Basin, Formcarry, Forminit, Web3Forms, Netlify Forms, Formspark, Form4Dev — with the tradeoffs each one carries. Written by someone who builds in this space and reads the competitor pages weekly.";
const publishedAt = "2026-05-16";
const updatedAt = "2026-05-16";

const fmtDate = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(iso + "T00:00:00Z").toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
    year: "numeric",
    month: "long",
    day: "numeric",
  });
---

<Base title={`${title} — Form4Dev`} description={description} current="blog">
  <article class="post container-wide">
    <header class="post-head">
      <a href="/blog" class="back mono">← Blog</a>
      <div class="post-meta mono">
        <time datetime={publishedAt}>{fmtDate(publishedAt)}</time>
        <span class="dot">·</span>
        <span>13 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="display">{title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Formspree has been the default answer for "how do I add a contact
        form to my static site" for a decade. It's a fine answer. It's
        not the only one anymore. Here's the honest landscape in 2026 —
        including the cases where I'd recommend a competitor over my own
        product.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="body">
      <h2>Disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        I build <a href="https://form4dev.com">Form4Dev</a>, one of the
        services on this list. The internet doesn't need another
        promotional listicle pretending to be neutral. I've tried to
        write this as the honest take I'd give a friend who emailed
        asking "which form backend should I use?" — including the cases
        where Formspree is still the right call and the cases where one
        of the other competitors fits better than Form4Dev.
      </p>

      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        If you want the safest, most documented option and don't mind
        paying past 50 submissions a month, stay on Formspree. If you'll
        live in the dashboard, look at Basin. If price is the dominant
        axis, Formcarry. If you submit forms from scripts or AI agents,
        Form4Dev. If you want unlimited free submissions and don't need a
        dashboard, Web3Forms. Below is the long version.
      </p>

      <h2>How to pick a form backend (the framework)</h2>
      <p>
        Form backends compete on a small number of axes. Picking one is
        about deciding which axis you weigh hardest — no service wins on
        all of them, and anyone who says theirs does is selling.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Maturity and ecosystem</strong> — how many integrations,
          how much documentation, how many third-party tutorials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dashboard UX</strong> — how nice the inbox is to live in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developer ergonomics</strong> — clear errors,
          predictable behaviour, framework libraries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Programmatic submission</strong> — whether
          scripts and AI agents can submit cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Email deliverability</strong> — notifications landing
          where you expect, Reply-To routing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Price at scale</strong> — what you pay at 1k, 10k, 100k
          submissions/month.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Self-hosting</strong> — whether you can run it
          yourself.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Below, I've ranked each alternative by best-fit-for-something,
        not by "best overall," which is a meaningless ranking when the
        right answer depends on what you're optimizing for.
      </p>

      <h2>The alternatives</h2>

      <h3>1. Basin — for designers and dashboard quality</h3>
      <p>
        Basin (<code>usebasin.com</code>) is the prettiest dashboard in
        the category by some distance. They've gone deep on visual
        polish, onboarding flow, and the kind of design choices that
        matter if you'll demo the dashboard to a client.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Basin wins</strong>: best-in-class UI, strong
        out-of-the-box spam filtering, generous 100/mo free tier with
        most features included, $8.25/mo starter that undercuts
        Formspree.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Basin loses</strong>: developer ergonomics
        are a secondary concern — docs lean on screenshots over
        code, and submissions from non-browser clients are a
        second-class path.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Basin if</strong>: you'll spend real time in the
        dashboard, you care about visual polish, and your forms are
        filled by humans in browsers.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Formcarry — for price and lifetime archive</h3>
      <p>
        Formcarry's edge is pricing: $6/mo starter (cheapest in the
        category), and the free tier keeps submissions in the lifetime
        archive instead of rotating them out after 30 days like most
        competitors.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formcarry wins</strong>: cheapest paid plan,
        lifetime archive on free tier, CSV + JSON export with no plan
        gating, file uploads on most plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formcarry loses</strong>: thinner product
        surface — fewer integrations, documentation leans
        reference rather than tutorial.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Formcarry if</strong>: price is the dominant axis
        and you want submissions archived forever without paying for
        retention.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Forminit (formerly Getform) — for static-site reliability</h3>
      <p>
        Getform rebranded to Forminit in January 2026. The product is
        the same well-tested form backend it's been for years — strong
        in the Hugo/Eleventy/Jekyll communities, good Zapier and webhook
        support, file uploads on every paid plan. We wrote a dedicated
        <a href="/blog/forminit-alternatives">Forminit alternatives
        piece</a> with more detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Forminit wins</strong>: rock-solid reliability,
        strong static-site-generator integration guides, mature
        deliverability.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Forminit loses</strong>: pricing is mid-pack
        ($9 starter, climbing to $24+), the dashboard is
        functional rather than delightful, and there's no
        first-class story for agent or automation traffic.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Forminit if</strong>: you want a boring, mature
        form backend with deep static-site-generator documentation, and
        you're not optimizing for programmatic submission.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Web3Forms — for unlimited free submissions</h3>
      <p>
        Web3Forms takes a different monetization tack: unlimited
        submissions on the free tier, most features open, paid tiers
        unlock dashboards and team features. If your form volume is
        unpredictable and you don't want to think about quota, this
        removes the worry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Web3Forms wins</strong>: unlimited free
        submissions, simple pricing, fast setup, good for prototypes
        and side projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Web3Forms loses</strong>: no dashboard on free
        tier (email notifications only), less battle-tested
        deliverability than Formspree-class services, integration depth
        is thinner.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Web3Forms if</strong>: you want a free form
        backend for a personal site or prototype and the
        email-only-no-dashboard flow is fine.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Netlify Forms — for Netlify-hosted sites</h3>
      <p>
        Netlify Forms is built into Netlify's hosting platform. If your
        site is already on Netlify, you can wire up a form by adding
        one attribute to your HTML — no third-party service to sign up
        for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Netlify Forms wins</strong>: zero-config when
        you're on Netlify, included in their plans up to a quota,
        native integration with Netlify Functions for custom logic.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Netlify Forms loses</strong>: locked to Netlify
        hosting — move and you rewrite. Recent shift to credit-based
        billing has made the cost less predictable. Not an option if
        you're on Vercel, Cloudflare Pages, or anywhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Netlify Forms if</strong>: you're already on
        Netlify, plan to stay, and your volume fits in the included
        quota. We have a longer piece on
        <a href="/blog/netlify-forms-alternatives">Netlify Forms
        alternatives</a> if you're shopping after a pricing surprise.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Formspark — for one-time-payment simplicity</h3>
      <p>
        Formspark uses a credit-pool model: $25 one-time buys 50,000
        submissions you use at your own pace. No recurring
        subscription. Strong fit for low-traffic sites where a monthly
        bill feels overweight.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspark wins</strong>: no recurring cost,
        simple mental model, great for low-traffic single-site setups.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspark loses</strong>: intentionally minimal
        feature surface, no first-class team features, less suited to
        anyone running multiple production sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Formspark if</strong>: you have one or two
        low-traffic sites and want to pay once for a multi-year
        submission budget.
      </p>

      <h3>7. Form4Dev — for agents, scripts, and flat pricing</h3>
      <p>
        Full disclosure: this is my product. I'll keep it short.
        Form4Dev is built from day one with non-browser
        submitters as first-class users — scripts, mobile apps,
        and AI agents — with transparent flat pricing ($0 / $9 /
        $29) and every feature on every plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Form4Dev wins</strong>: cleanest path for
        scripted and agent submission, every feature available
        on the free tier (capped at 100 submissions/month),
        built-in support for sending notifications from your own
        domain, flat pricing with no feature gating between
        plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Form4Dev loses</strong>: we're early. No
        decade-old integration catalog, no Formspree-tier
        tutorial corpus, less brand recognition. Product is
        production-ready; ecosystem is younger.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Form4Dev if</strong>: you submit forms from
        scripts, mobile apps, or AI agents; you value flat
        pricing; you want every capability available on a free
        trial.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-side at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Service</th>
            <th>Free tier</th>
            <th>Starter</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Formspree</td><td>50/mo</td><td>$10/mo</td><td>Maturity, integrations</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Basin</td><td>100/mo</td><td>$8.25/mo</td><td>UI quality</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Formcarry</td><td>100/mo (lifetime)</td><td>$6/mo</td><td>Price + archive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forminit</td><td>50/mo</td><td>$9/mo</td><td>Static-site reliability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Web3Forms</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>$10/mo (dashboard)</td><td>Free unlimited</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Netlify Forms</td><td>Included on plan</td><td>Tied to hosting</td><td>Netlify-hosted sites</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Formspark</td><td>—</td><td>$25 one-time / 50k</td><td>No subscription</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Form4Dev</td><td>100/mo, all features</td><td>$9/mo</td><td>Agents, scripts, flat pricing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted-note">
        Pricing accurate as of {fmtDate(updatedAt)} based on each
        service's public pricing page. Verify before committing —
        pricing changes more often than blog posts get updated.
      </p>

      <h2>Where Formspree is still the right call</h2>
      <p>
        It's easy to write a "leave Formspree, here's something better"
        listicle. It's more honest to admit when Formspree is still the
        right answer.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>You need an integration that only Formspree has.</strong>
          They have the deepest catalog by a wide margin. If you need a
          specific destination (a particular CRM, an obscure tool) and
          Formspree supports it natively, that's worth paying for.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You're learning, and want the most-documented option.</strong>
          Every JavaScript framework tutorial mentions Formspree first.
          That ecosystem matters when you're stuck at 11pm.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You've already integrated <code>@formspree/react</code></strong>
          and it works. Switching costs time. If nothing's broken,
          don't switch just to switch.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The point of this post isn't to talk you off Formspree. It's
        to make sure you know what else exists if Formspree isn't
        fitting.
      </p>

      <h2>How to actually migrate</h2>
      <p>
        Form backends are easy to switch. The work is mostly clerical.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Export your existing submissions.</strong> Every
          service supports CSV export. Do this first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sign up for the new service and recreate your forms.</strong>
          Match field names so downstream automations don't break.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Update the <code>action=</code> URL on every form.</strong>
          If you used an environment variable, this is one change. If
          you hardcoded the endpoint everywhere, keep a list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test in staging.</strong> Submit each form, verify
          the notification arrives, verify webhooks fire.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ship.</strong> Leave the old service active for a
          week in case traffic routes there. Then cancel.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Total time for most sites: under an afternoon.</p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>

      <h3>What's the best Formspree alternative in 2026?</h3>
      <p>
        Depends on what you weigh. Basin is the best UI, Formcarry is
        the cheapest, Forminit is the most reliable static-site option,
        Web3Forms is the best free tier on volume, Form4Dev is the
        best for AI agents and programmatic submission. There isn't a
        single "best" — pick on the axis that matters to you.
      </p>

      <h3>Is there a free Formspree alternative with unlimited submissions?</h3>
      <p>
        Web3Forms offers unlimited submissions on its free tier, with
        the caveat that you only get email notifications — no
        dashboard. For most personal sites and prototypes, that's
        fine.
      </p>

      <h3>Which form backend is best for AI agents?</h3>
      <p>
        Of the services here, Form4Dev is built specifically for
        agent submission as a first-class case. We wrote a
        deeper piece on
        <a href="/blog/form-backend-for-ai-agents">form
        backends for AI agents</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Are there open-source Formspree alternatives?</h3>
      <p>
        FormGrid is the most active open-source form backend at time of
        writing — you run it on your own infrastructure. None of the
        hosted services here offer first-party self-hosting today.
      </p>

      <h3>How much will I pay at 10,000 submissions/month?</h3>
      <p>
        Roughly: Formspree $35-50, Basin $25, Formcarry $15-30,
        Forminit $24, Web3Forms $10 (with dashboard), Form4Dev $29.
        Check each on the current pricing page before deciding.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I keep using Formspree's React library with another backend?</h3>
      <p>
        No — <code>@formspree/react</code> is Formspree-specific. If
        you switch, you'll either use the new service's library (most
        have one), or write a small fetch wrapper yourself. It's about
        20 lines of TypeScript.
      </p>

      <h2>The takeaway</h2>
      <p>
        The form-backend category isn't winner-take-all. Formspree
        earned its position as the default by being the most mature
        and most documented option for a decade — that doesn't
        disappear because newer services exist. What's changed is
        there are now real specialists worth knowing about: dashboard
        quality, lifetime archive, unlimited free, agent submission,
        one-time pricing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most people will look around and stay on Formspree. That's
        fine. The point is to look around with an honest map.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the agent-and-API-first angle is what brought you here,
        <a href="https://form4dev.com">Form4Dev</a> is built around it.
        <a href={`${APP}/register`} class="plausible-event-name=Signup+CTA plausible-event-location=blog-formspree-alts plausible-event-plan=free">
          Start free
        </a>
        — every feature unlocked, capped at 100 submissions/month, no
        credit card.
      </p>

      <p class="muted-note last-updated">
        Last updated {fmtDate(updatedAt)}. Pricing changes — spotted
        something out of date? Email
        <a href="mailto:hello@form4dev.com">hello@form4dev.com</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

